  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1021 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nAfter a semi-active weather day across portions of the Mid-South,\nwe have trended calmer over the past few hours. Taking a look at\nthe latest KNQA radar sweep, a few light showers exist over\nnorthwest Mississippi and the Delta region with much of the\nconvection to our south. Isolated to scattered shower and\nthunderstorm chances will remain across areas mainly south of the\nTennessee and Mississippi state line through the overnight hours.\nWhile areas north will remain largely rainfree. The severe\nweather window has primarily closed for the evening. Narrow\ndewpoint depressions, saturated soils, and calm winds along a low-\nlevel inversion will lead to pretty decent fog potential\novernight. Though Dense Fog is not anticipated at this time,\nthose with an early morning commute should use extra caution. Any\nfog that does develop will begin to burn off shortly after\nsunrise as mixing occurs.\n\nHeading into the daytime hours tomorrow, we're looking pretty dry\nacross the Mid-South, however, as we remain under a quasi-\nnorthwest flow regime, a few afternoon pop-up thunderstorms can't\nbe ruled out, especially with peak daytime heating. By Friday,\ndrier air will begin to filter in from the Midwest, drying us out\nand slightly decreasing our dewpoints. This will be the trend\nthrough at least midweek as upper-level subtropical ridging,\ncurrently centered over the four corners region, begins to build\nand broaden east. With a broadening ridge, comes a warming trend.\nWe'll begin flirting with above normal temperatures by Saturday\nwith highs in the mid 90s. Come Monday, ensemble members are\nhinting at the aforementioned ridge nearly center over us at a\nforecasted 594dam. Heat Advisory potential will be evaluated on a\nday to day basis next week as dewpoints will primarily drive\n105+F heat indice potential. The latest 8-14 day outlook has a 70-\n80% chance of us leaning above normal moving through the first\nweek of September. The dog days of summer are not over.\n\nAEH",
